open your eyes real wide close real  tight  they are the fundamental movements we  all take for granted  but one's joe dimeo is now having to  relearn  double vision at all when you look side  to side his face  and hands used to belong to someone else  which means everything from blinking  to gripping is once again entirely new  great squeeze my fingers really tight  in an interview with sky news the 22  year old from new jersey  explained what it's like to undergo such  a dramatic  transformation i have to relearn  everything  you know so like when a baby is born  all it does is plays with toys and you  know just keeps moving his hands around  so  that's exactly what i have to do i just  got to keep moving my hands  this is what joe used to look like  before a car crash nearly ended his life  two years ago  the burns he suffered were horrific  multiple skin grafts and reconstructive  surgeries  unable to restore his vision nor the use  of his hands  dozens of doctors were involved in the  transplant operation in new york which  took 23 hours  the procedure has only ever been  attempted twice before  but neither time successful having come  out the other side  joe says he's never looked back i kind  of just you know i just kind of jumped  right into it  i saw my face at first i was like this  is this is me now  and i just i just kind of went with it  that's to put it mildly  less than six months on he's already  practicing his golf swing  his doctors are understandably excited  this confirms to us that we can  transplant this amount of tissue  reliably and safely  with great success this this provides a  great deal of hope  for so many patients that we get to  evaluate in our clinics that we would  tell them there's nothing that we could  do  for joe it's the little things that now  count for a lot like being reunited with  his dog buster  who seemingly didn't notice a change i  guess he recognized the smell  of me because he just he ran right to me  out of everyone